# Tracked example for /opt/orchestration/observer_run.env, which
# startup_machine.sh seeds from this file on first boot and never touches again
# (mirrors how .env.example seeds .env -- see that file's comment in
# startup_machine.sh for why "only seed once" matters).
#
# The four values below are explicit, human-chosen identifiers for the run the
# cron-driven `pipeline-supervisor observe` wakeup should watch. None of them can
# be safely derived from Airflow or from unified_pipeline.yaml:
#
#   - OBSERVER_ISSUE is the GitHub issue the observer comments on. Nothing in
#     the repo names it; it is opened by hand (or by a future config PR) per run.
#   - OBSERVER_RUN is the run's own prefix in the runs bucket, as `diff --run`.
#     It is left unset here on purpose: a fresh VM is not yet watching any run.
#   - OBSERVER_REFERENCE is a previously published release to diff the run
#     against once it reaches a terminal state, as `diff --reference` (e.g.
#     '26.03'). It is NOT unified_pipeline.yaml's `release_name` -- that field
#     is where THIS run would write if it were a production run, not a prior
#     baseline (see cli.py's module docstring and the design ledger's Task 5
#     entry). A reference is always a human judgement call.
#   - OBSERVER_REFERENCE_BUCKET is which bucket that release lives in, as
#     `diff --reference-bucket`. Leave it blank to use the default,
#     open-targets-pre-data-releases. Set it when the release you chose is not
#     there: releases reach that bucket first and the public
#     open-targets-data-releases later, and the two do not hold the same set --
#     as of 2026-08-25 the pre-releases bucket stops at 26.03 while 26.06 exists
#     only in the public one. Naming a release the bucket does not have is not
#     an error: the comparison finds nothing on the reference side and reports
#     every dataset as "present in the run only", which reads as a dramatic
#     finding rather than as a misconfiguration.
#
# OBSERVER_RUN and OBSERVER_REFERENCE are only used together: `observe` skips
# the terminal-state dataset comparison unless both are set. Leaving either
# blank does not stop the observer from journalling stalls, failures and
# completions and commenting on them -- only the dataset diff is skipped.
#
# Edit /opt/orchestration/observer_run.env directly on the VM when a new run
# starts; this .example file is what a fresh VM seeds it from, not itself read.
#
# Update OBSERVER_ISSUE and OBSERVER_RUN TOGETHER. Because this file is seeded
# once and then never touched again, it survives into the next run carrying the
# last one's identity. That happened on do/platform-2609-1: it still read 4505 /
# do/platform-2608-3, so the observer reported the new run onto the finished
# run's issue and diffed against the wrong bucket, and nobody noticed until the
# comments turned up on the closed issue.
#
# observer_wrapper.sh now refuses to run when OBSERVER_RUN disagrees with
# `run_name` in the unified_pipeline.yaml the same checkout is serving. It exits
# non-zero and says so on stderr rather than skipping quietly -- a quiet skip
# looks exactly like a healthy run, since the observer is silent most of the
# time. OBSERVER_ISSUE cannot be checked this way; nothing a run produces knows
# its issue number. Validating the one identifier that is checkable is what
# catches the stale file, so keep the two edits in the same change.

OBSERVER_ISSUE=
OBSERVER_RUN=
OBSERVER_REFERENCE=
OBSERVER_REFERENCE_BUCKET=
